Mozilla, Shopify, and a coalition representing up to 55,000 European Union businesses and organizations are urging the European Commission to take enforcement action against Apple over restrictions on competing iPhone browsers.<br/>
<br/>
<img src="https://images.macrumors.com/article-new/2025/03/european-commission.jpg" alt="" width="1600" height="900" class="aligncenter size-full wp-image-995806" /><br/>
In an <a href="https://action.open-web-advocacy.org/">open letter</a> to Commission President Ursula von der Leyen, the coalition argues that Apple's changes under the Digital Markets Act (DMA) have failed to deliver meaningful browser competition. According to organizer Open Web Advocacy, no browser vendor has been able to switch its existing EU users from Apple's WebKit engine to its own engine more than two and a half years after Apple's compliance deadline. Signatories include Vivaldi, Vercel, Y Combinator, the European DIGITAL SME Alliance, European Publishers Council, News Media Europe, and European Digital Rights. <br/>
<br/>
Apple <a href="https://www.macrumors.com/2024/01/25/third-party-default-browsers-eu-ios-17-4/">began allowing alternative browser engines</a> in the EU with iOS 17.4, but the coalition says its conditions make that permission impractical to use. Browser engines power websites and web apps, determining their performance and available features.<br/>
<br/>
Mozilla <a href="https://blog.mozilla.org/en/mozilla/internet-policy/apple-ios-browser-engine-rules/">outlined the problem</a> in a separate statement on November 20. It says bringing its Gecko engine to iOS would require releasing a second Firefox app rather than updating the existing one. Users would then have to download that app, transfer their bookmarks, passwords, and history, and switch manually.<br/>
<br/>
Mozilla said its efforts to improve Apple's conditions have largely failed. While daily Firefox use on iOS has doubled following the EU's browser choice changes, its users still rely on Apple's WebKit engine. The coalition argues that these restrictions also prevent web apps from becoming stronger competitors to <a href="https://www.macrumors.com/guide/app-store/">App Store</a> apps, limiting businesses' ability to reach customers without depending on Apple's store.<br/>
<br/>
Apple's <a href="https://developer.apple.com/support/alternative-browser-engines/">requirements</a> say competing engines must meet privacy and security standards, including timely vulnerability fixes. The company says these conditions protect users from malicious web content.<br/>
<br/>
